Absolutely! Here’s a simple example of how to control a servo using an ESP32 with Arduino IDE. This code sweeps the servo connected to pin 27 from 0° to 180° and back:

```cpp
#include <ESP32Servo.h>

Servo myServo;  // create servo object

void setup() {
  myServo.attach(27);  // attach servo to GPIO 27
}

void loop() {
  // Sweep from 0 to 180 degrees
  for (int pos = 0; pos <= 180; pos += 1) {
    myServo.write(pos);
    delay(15);
  }
  // Sweep back from 180 to 0 degrees
  for (int pos = 180; pos >= 0; pos -= 1) {
    myServo.write(pos);
    delay(15);
  }
}
```

**Instructions:**
1. Make sure you installed the [ESP32Servo library](https://github.com/jkb-git/ESP32Servo) in the Arduino IDE (`Sketch > Include Library > Manage Libraries`).
2. Wire the servo signal pin to GPIO 27, and power the servo separately if possible (the ESP32's onboard regulator may not be enough for some servos).
3. Upload the code and enjoy the sweep!

